
Exposing custom metrics from a python application

s1: Install prometheus-client
s2: Do python code changes in app.py
        from prometheus_client import generate_latest
        from prometheus_client import CONTENT_TYPE_LATEST
        from flask import Response

        @app.route("/metrics")
        def metrics():
            return Response(generate_latest(), mimetype=CONTENT_TYPE_LATEST)


s3: Build docker image again & apply in k8s deployment
s4: kubectl exec -it <pod> -n <namespace> -- sh
    curl localhost:7008/metrics


s5: To expose it in prometheus targets


1. Create serviceMonitor & it selects Services using labels
      A ServiceMonitor refers to the port name, not the port number.

apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: ServiceMonitor
metadata:
  name: logapp-monitor
  namespace: monitoring
  labels:
    release: monitoring            //Get from kube-prometheus-stack installation cmd
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: ml_logapp

  namespaceSelector:
    matchNames:
    - logapp

  endpoints:
  - port: http
    path: /metrics
    interval: 15s


2. Service

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: logapp-service
  labels:
    app: ml_logapp
spec:
  selector:
    app: ml_logapp
  ports:
  - name: http
    protocol: TCP
    port: 80
    targetPort: 7008
  type: NodePort



3. Deployment

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: logapp-deployment
  labels:
    app: logapp
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: ml_logapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: ml_logapp
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: logapp-container
        image: saivarma5557/logapp:v2
        ports:
        - containerPort: 7008



from browser: http://<publicip>:8080/metrics 
from prometheus: http://10.244.0.17:7008/metrics


1. Browser access:
You access:

http://13.126.95.151:8080/metrics

Flow:

Your Laptop Browser
        │
        ▼
AWS EC2 Public IP
13.126.95.151
        │
        ▼
NodePort (8080)
        │
        ▼
Service
        │
        ▼
Pod
        │
        ▼
7008 (/metrics)

Here:

13.126.95.151 = EC2 Public IP
8080 = NodePort
7008 = Container port

Your browser cannot directly access Pod IPs, so it goes through the Kubernetes Service.


2. Prometheus access:
Prometheus uses

http://10.244.0.17:7008/metrics

Flow:

Prometheus Pod
      │
      ▼
Pod IP
10.244.0.17
      │
      ▼
Container Port 7008

Since Prometheus is inside the same Kubernetes cluster, it doesn't need the public IP or NodePort.

It communicates directly with the Pod over the cluster network.


ARCH:

                  Internet

                       │

             13.126.95.151:8080

                       │

                NodePort Service

                       │

        ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐

        ▼              ▼              ▼

10.244.0.17      10.244.0.18      10.244.0.19
   Pod               Pod              Pod
    │                 │                │
7008/metrics      7008/metrics     7008/metrics


Your browser enters from the top.

Prometheus is already inside the cluster, so it starts at the pod layer.
